GigE Vision
===========

This library allows a Circle application to control GigE Vision cameras and to
receive their images over Ethernet. The Raspberry Pi acts as the application
(host) side of the GigE Vision standard. The library is written from scratch
and does not depend on third-party code.

FEATURES

* GVCP (GigE Vision Control Protocol, UDP port 3956)
  - Device discovery (broadcast DISCOVERY_CMD)
  - FORCEIP_CMD to assign a temporary IP address to a camera, which is not
    reachable from our subnet (e.g. uses a link-local 169.254.x.x address)
  - READREG/WRITEREG, READMEM/WRITEMEM with transparent splitting, alignment,
    retries (same request ID) and PENDING_ACK handling
  - Control channel privilege (control or exclusive access) and a heartbeat
    task, which keeps the privilege alive
  - PACKETRESEND_CMD
* GVSP (GigE Vision Streaming Protocol)
  - Reassembly of blocks (frames) from leader, payload and trailer packets
  - Image leader information (pixel format, size, offset, padding, timestamp)
  - Detection of lost packets, optional resend requests (grouped in ranges)
  - Handling of lost trailers, late/stale packets, block ID wrap-around and
    devices, which restart their block IDs
  - Standard and extended (64-bit) block IDs (resend with standard IDs only)
* GenICam (subset of GenApi)
  - Download of the device description file from the device ("Local:" URL),
    plain XML or zipped (own DEFLATE decompressor with CRC check)
  - Built-in XML parser
  - Feature access by name: Integer, IntReg, MaskedIntReg, StructReg/
    StructEntry, Float, FloatReg, Boolean, Enumeration/EnumEntry, Command,
    String, StringReg, IntSwissKnife, SwissKnife, IntConverter, Converter,
    Category, pIsImplemented/pIsAvailable
  - Formula evaluator for SwissKnife/Converter nodes (all operators, functions
    and constants of the GenICam standard, pVariable/Constant/Expression)
  - Address calculation with multiple Address/pAddress, pIndex (Offset/pOffset)
    and embedded IntSwissKnife, byte order and bit numbering of BigEndian and
    LittleEndian registers, signed values

CLASSES

CGigECamera		High-level camera class (open with control privilege and
			heartbeat, load description, configure stream channel,
			start/stop acquisition). Start here.
CGVCPClient		GVCP control channel (register/memory access), static
			Discover() and ForceIP() methods
CGVSPReceiver		GVSP stream receiver, returns complete frames
CGenICamNodeMap		GenICam feature access (GetInteger(), SetEnum(),
			ExecuteCommand() etc.)
CGenICamFormula		Formula evaluator
CXMLDocument		Minimal XML parser
CUnzip			ZIP extraction and DEFLATE decompressor
gvcp.h			Protocol definitions (commands, bootstrap registers, packet
			layouts, pixel formats)

USAGE

	#include <gigevision/gigecamera.h>

	TGigEDeviceInfo Devices[4];
	int nDevices = CGVCPClient::Discover (&m_Net, Devices, 4);

	CGigECamera Camera (&m_Net);
	Camera.Open (Devices[0].IPAddress);
	Camera.LoadDescription ();

	CGenICamNodeMap *pNodeMap = Camera.GetNodeMap ();
	pNodeMap->SetEnum ("PixelFormat", "Mono8");
	pNodeMap->SetInteger ("Width", 640);
	pNodeMap->SetFloat ("ExposureTime", 5000.0);

	size_t nSize = Camera.GetPayloadSize ();
	u8 *pBuffer = new u8[nSize];

	Camera.OpenStream ();
	Camera.StartAcquisition ();

	TGVSPFrameInfo Info;
	if (   Camera.GetStream ()->ReceiveFrame (&Info, pBuffer, nSize, 1000)
	    && Info.Complete)
	{
		// Info.SizeX x Info.SizeY pixels of Info.PixelFormat in pBuffer
	}

	Camera.StopAcquisition ();
	Camera.Close ();

The library needs the network subsystem and the scheduler (the heartbeat runs
as a separate task). Calls into the library must be made from tasks on core 0,
like all calls into the Circle network stack.

BUILDING

Build the Circle libraries first (./makeall in the Circle root directory), then:

	cd addon/gigevision
	make

Link libgigevision.a into your application before the Circle libraries, see
sample/Makefile.

NOTES AND LIMITATIONS

* Circle does not support jumbo frames. The packet size is limited to 1500
  bytes, which is set by CGigECamera::OpenStream().
* The throughput of the Circle network stack is limited. With cameras, which
  can deliver more data than the Raspberry Pi can receive, reduce the frame
  rate, the image size (ROI, binning) or increase the inter-packet delay with
  CGigECamera::SetPacketDelay() to avoid lost packets.
* A camera must be on the same IP subnet as the Raspberry Pi, otherwise its
  packets are sent to the default gateway. Use CGVCPClient::ForceIP() in this
  case (the sample program does this automatically).
* Only "Local:" description file URLs are supported. With "File:" or "http:"
  URLs load the XML otherwise and pass it to CGigECamera::LoadDescription
  (pXML, nLength).
* Not supported: message channel/events, action commands, chunk data (nodes on
  a chunk port fail to read), multi-part/H.264/JPEG payload reassembly
  (payload packets of these types are ignored), GenICam value caching,
  selectors beyond plain register indexing (pIndex), pIsLocked checks.
* Float values are formatted with CString::Format ("%.3f").

TESTING

The library was tested on a Linux host with a port of the Circle socket API
against the "arv-fake-gv-camera" simulator of the Aravis project (discovery,
control, description download, feature access, streaming with heartbeat and
block ID wrap-around, packet loss), and with unit tests of the XML parser,
the ZIP/DEFLATE decompressor, the formula evaluator, the GenICam node map and
the GVSP receiver including resend handling.
